# panfs-tenant-operator

{{ template "chart.description" . }}

{{ template "chart.badgesSection" . }}

## What the PanFS Tenant operator is

The PanFS Tenant operator runs once per cluster, as one pod, next to the PanFS CSI driver.
It serves two resources of the API group `panfs.vdura.com/v1alpha1`:

| Resource            | What the operator does for it                                                               |
|:--------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `TenantNetwork`     | Puts Kubernetes nodes on the network of a PanFS tenant, and reports which nodes are ready   |
| `PanfsStorageClass` | Creates a StorageClass of the PanFS CSI driver, and lets the CSI controller read its Secret |

The charts of this repository fit together like this:

| Chart                                             | Installed       | What it creates                                      |
|:--------------------------------------------------|:----------------|:-----------------------------------------------------|
| [`csi-panfs`](../panfs/Readme.md)                 | once            | The PanFS CSI driver                                 |
| `panfs-tenant-operator` (this chart)              | once            | The operator and the two resource definitions        |
| [`panfs-tenant`](../tenant/Readme.md)             | once per tenant | The realm Secret and the `TenantNetwork` of a tenant |
| [`panfs-storageclass`](../storageclass/Readme.md) | once per class  | One `PanfsStorageClass`                              |

A cluster whose nodes reach the realm without a tenant network needs only the `PanfsStorageClass` part;
see [A realm without a tenant network](#a-realm-without-a-tenant-network). The whole operator is installed there too,
and its tenant network part has nothing to do.

## Tenant network

A volume of a PanFS tenant is reachable only from the network of that tenant.
The operator puts Kubernetes nodes on that network, and it tells Kubernetes which nodes are ready.

After the operator is installed, a tenant needs two small things in one namespace:
a Secret with the PanAPI address and the token of the tenant, and a `TenantNetwork` that names the Secret
and selects the nodes:

```yaml
apiVersion: panfs.vdura.com/v1alpha1
kind: TenantNetwork
metadata:
  name: acme-network
  namespace: acme
spec:
  realmSecretName: acme-credentials
  nodeSelector:
    storage-network: acme
```

For every `TenantNetwork` the operator does the following:

1. Reads the Secret and asks PanAPI which tenant the token belongs to. You do not write the tenant name anywhere.
2. Creates a DaemonSet in the namespace of the resource. Its pods run on the nodes that `nodeSelector` selects.
3. Reports the result in the status of the resource and as events on it.

Two node labels are involved:

| Label                                        | Who sets it  | Meaning                                      |
|----------------------------------------------|--------------|----------------------------------------------|
| The labels of `nodeSelector`                 | You          | The node is meant for this tenant            |
| `panfs.vdura.com/tenant-network=<namespace>` | The node pod | The network of the tenant works on this node |

The value of the second label is the namespace of the `TenantNetwork`. It is the label for placing workloads,
and the status of the resource shows it as `managedTopology`, with its key and its value:

```bash
kubectl -n acme get tenantnetwork acme-network -o jsonpath='{.status.managedTopology}'
```

```text
{"key":"panfs.vdura.com/tenant-network","value":"acme"}
```

Without `nodeSelector`, the pods run on all nodes of the cluster, the control plane nodes included:

```yaml
spec:
  realmSecretName: acme-credentials
```

A node pod does the following on its node, and repeats it on an interval:

1. Attaches the node to the subnet of the tenant through PanAPI. A repeated attach does not move the node.
2. Builds the tenant interface, or corrects it in place. An interface that is already right is not touched.
   The realm decides the kind: `vxlan<id>` on a VXLAN segment, `vlan<id>` on a VLAN segment; see [Segment types](#segment-types).
3. Moves the interface to the firewalld zone `trusted`, when firewalld runs on the node.
4. Checks that a director of the realm answers on the tenant interface.
5. Sets the second label on the node when the check passes, and removes it when the check fails.

A node pod is Ready only while the check passes.

When a node leaves the tenant, its pod cleans up: it removes the ready label, removes the tenant interface,
and detaches the node from the subnet in the realm. A node leaves the tenant when it no longer matches `nodeSelector`
(you removed its label, or you changed the selector), or when the `TenantNetwork` is deleted. A restart or an upgrade of the pods is not a removal:
the interface and the attachment stay, so mounted volumes keep working.

> **Note:** Installation into the `default` namespace is not permitted. You must specify a non-default namespace using the `--namespace <namespace>` option during installation.

### Resources Created by the Helm Chart

- The `CustomResourceDefinition` of `TenantNetwork` (`tenantnetworks.panfs.vdura.com`)
  and of `PanfsStorageClass` (`panfsstorageclasses.panfs.vdura.com`, short name `psc`).
- A `Deployment` with one pod: the operator. With the release name of this guide it is `panfs-tenant-operator`.
- A `ServiceAccount`, a `ClusterRole` and a `ClusterRoleBinding` for the operator.
- A `ClusterRole` for the node pods: read a node and patch its labels.

### Resources Created by the Operator

For every `TenantNetwork`, in the namespace of the resource:

- A `DaemonSet` with the name `panfs-tenant-network-<name>` that runs the node pods.
- A `ServiceAccount`, a `Role` and a `RoleBinding` for these pods. The pods read their own DaemonSet when they stop,
  to tell a removal from a restart.
- A `ClusterRoleBinding` that binds the `ClusterRole` of the node pods to that ServiceAccount.

The operator does not create the Secret.

For every `PanfsStorageClass`:

- A `StorageClass` with the name of the resource.
- A `Role` and a `RoleBinding` named `panfs-storage-class-<NAME>` in the namespace of the resource:
  the CSI controller may read the one Secret that the StorageClass names.

## Requirements

- **PanFS CSI driver:** the driver is installed. The operator reads the namespace of the driver from the CSIDriver
  object `com.vdura.csi.panfs`.

The other requirements are for a `TenantNetwork`. A cluster that uses only `PanfsStorageClass` with
`spec.realmSecretName` needs a Secret with the keys `api` and `token` in the namespace of the resource, and nothing else.

- **PanFS realm:** the tenant has a subnet on a VXLAN or a VLAN segment; see [Segment types](#segment-types).
- **Secret:** a Secret in the namespace of the `TenantNetwork` with the keys `api` and `token`, and optionally `ca_cert`.
  The chart [`panfs-tenant`](../tenant/Readme.md) creates such a Secret.
- **Token permissions**, all for its own tenant: read the tenant; list the subnets and read the mount recipe of a subnet;
  list, read, create and delete attachments of a subnet.
- **PanAPI is reachable from the operator pod.** The operator runs on the pod network and calls PanAPI
  to learn the tenant of a token.
- **Tenant namespace:** the namespace of a `TenantNetwork` must allow pods that use the host network and a `hostPath` volume
  (Pod Security level `privileged`). The operator pod itself has no such need.
- **One node, one tenant:** a node can be on the network of one tenant only, so a node must match the `nodeSelector`
  of one `TenantNetwork` only. The operator checks this when it serves a resource: a `TenantNetwork` that selects
  a node of another one gets the phase `Error` and no node pods. Two resources without a `nodeSelector` both select
  every node, so the second one is refused; give each tenant its own nodes.
  The check does not see a node whose labels change later, so keep the labels of the tenants apart.
- **The interface name is reserved:** the tenant interface is `vxlan<VNI>` or `vlan<VLAN id>`. The node pod marks
  the interface it creates with the alias `panfs-tenant-network`. When a node already has an interface with that name
  and without the alias, the pod does not change or remove it; the node stays `NotReady` with the reason.
- **One `TenantNetwork` per tenant and per namespace:** a namespace holds one `TenantNetwork`, because its name
  is the value of the ready label, and a tenant is served by one `TenantNetwork`. The one that runs its node pods
  keeps the tenant; without a running one, the oldest gets it. The operator reports an error on every other one.
- **Calico:** with `IP_AUTODETECTION_METHOD` left at its default, Calico can move a node to its new tenant address,
  and the BGP sessions between the nodes then fail. Pin Calico to the node address before you label the first node:

  ```bash
  kubectl -n kube-system set env daemonset/calico-node IP_AUTODETECTION_METHOD=kubernetes-internal-ip
  kubectl -n kube-system rollout status daemonset/calico-node
  ```

  Other network plugins that pick the node address by themselves need the same care. The operator does not change the network plugin.

### Segment types

The realm places the subnets of its tenants on one kind of network segment, and PanAPI tells every node which one:
the record of a node on the subnet carries the segment type and the segment id. You do not configure the kind.

| Segment type | Tenant interface of a node                                 | What the node needs                                      |
|--------------|------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| `vxlan`      | `vxlan<VNI>`, a tunnel over the underlay network           | UDP to the realm nodes on the tunnel port                |
| `vlan`       | `vlan<VLAN id>`, an 802.1Q interface on a parent interface | The switch ports of the node must carry that VLAN        |
| `none`       | None                                                       | Not supported; the node stays `NotReady` with the reason |

On a VLAN segment the parent interface is the interface of the node that has its underlay address.
To use another one, name it: the value `vlanParentInterface` of the chart for all tenants,
or `spec.vlanParentInterface` of a `TenantNetwork` for one tenant.
The operator cannot configure or check the switches: when the VLAN does not reach a node,
its interface is built, but no director answers, and the node stays `NotReady`.

> **Note:** VLAN segments are covered by unit tests and by a check of the interface commands on a node.
> They are not yet validated against a realm that uses VLAN segments.

### Security

Treat the operator and the node pods as trusted, cluster-wide components. Who may create a `TenantNetwork`
in a namespace gets their effect on the selected nodes. One ServiceAccount holds the permissions for both resources.

- **The node pods are equal to root on their node.** The container is not marked privileged, and it drops all
  capabilities except `NET_ADMIN` and `NET_RAW`. But it uses the host network, it runs as root, and it reaches
  the system D-Bus of the node to set the firewalld zone; root on that bus can ask systemd to run commands.
  This is why the namespace of a `TenantNetwork` needs the Pod Security level `privileged`.
- **The node pods can change any node.** Their ServiceAccount may read and patch every node of the cluster,
  not only its own, because Kubernetes cannot limit this permission to one node or to one label.
  Who may create pods in the namespace of a `TenantNetwork` can run a pod with that ServiceAccount,
  so give that right only to people who may change nodes.
- **The operator reads Secrets in every namespace.** Its `ClusterRole` allows `get` on Secrets in the whole
  cluster, because a `TenantNetwork` and a `PanfsStorageClass` can be in any namespace. It cannot list Secrets,
  and it reads only the Secrets that these resources name. Who may create a `TenantNetwork` in a namespace can make
  the operator use a Secret of that namespace. For a `PanfsStorageClass` the operator only checks that the Secret
  exists; it needs the permission because Kubernetes lets it give the CSI controller only a permission that it holds itself.
- **A `PanfsStorageClass` opens one Secret to the CSI controller.** Who may create a `PanfsStorageClass` in a namespace
  lets the CSI controller read one Secret of that namespace, and creates a StorageClass for the whole cluster.
  Give this permission like the one to create a StorageClass.
- **The operator can change any node.** Its `ClusterRole` allows `list` and `patch` on nodes. It uses the patch
  only to remove the ready label and the annotations of the node pods that a deleted `TenantNetwork` left behind.
- **The operator creates workloads in every namespace.** It may create DaemonSets, ServiceAccounts, Roles and
  RoleBindings wherever a `TenantNetwork` is, and it may create and delete ClusterRoleBindings.
  It may also create, change and delete StorageClasses, and the Role and the RoleBinding of a `PanfsStorageClass`.
- **The firewalld zone `trusted` opens the node to the tenant network.** Every port of the node is then reachable
  from the tenant subnet. Use another zone with `firewalldZone` to narrow this.

## Usage

### 1. Install the operator

Once per cluster, in the namespace of the PanFS CSI driver (`csi-panfs` in the examples of this repository):

```bash
helm upgrade --install panfs-tenant-operator ./ \
    --namespace csi-panfs \
    --wait
```

The operator works in any other namespace too: it finds the driver through the CSIDriver object.

#### Using Helm overrides:

```yaml
# overrides.yaml
# Only for an image in a private registry. The Secret must exist in the namespace of the operator
# and in the namespace of every TenantNetwork.
imagePullSecrets:
  - my-registry-secret
```

```bash
helm upgrade --install panfs-tenant-operator ./ \
    --namespace csi-panfs \
    --values overrides.yaml \
    --wait
```

### 2. Label the nodes of the tenant

Any label works; the `TenantNetwork` selects the nodes by it. Skip this step to use all nodes of the cluster:

```bash
kubectl label node <NODE_NAME> <LABEL_KEY>=<LABEL_VALUE>
```

### 3. Create the Secret and the TenantNetwork of the tenant

```bash
kubectl create namespace <NAMESPACE>

# The credentials of the tenant
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> create secret generic <SECRET_NAME> \
    --from-literal=api=<PANAPI_ADDRESS> \
    --from-literal=token=<TOKEN>

cat <<EOF | kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> apply -f -
apiVersion: panfs.vdura.com/v1alpha1
kind: TenantNetwork
metadata:
  name: <NAME>
spec:
  realmSecretName: <SECRET_NAME>
  nodeSelector:
    <LABEL_KEY>: <LABEL_VALUE>
EOF
```

With Helm, the [PanFS Tenant chart](../tenant/Readme.md) does this step in one release: it creates the Secret
and the `TenantNetwork`, and it checks first that this operator is installed.

The Secret of a [PanFS StorageClass](../storageclass/Readme.md) release has the keys that the operator needs too.
To use it, create the `TenantNetwork` in the namespace of that release and name its Secret; the Secret has the name of the release.

Fields of `spec`:

| Field                 | Required | Meaning                                                                                |
|-----------------------|----------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `realmSecretName`     | Yes      | Name of the Secret with the PanAPI address and the token of the tenant                 |
| `nodeSelector`        | No       | Labels that select the nodes of the tenant; without it, all nodes of the cluster       |
| `vlanParentInterface` | No       | Interface that carries the VLAN on a VLAN segment; see [Segment types](#segment-types) |
| `interval`            | No       | Replaces the value `interval` of the chart for this tenant                             |
| `firewalldZone`       | No       | Replaces the value `firewalldZone` of the chart for this tenant                        |
| `detachOnRemoval`     | No       | Replaces the value `detachOnRemoval` of the chart for this tenant                      |

### 4. Check the result

```bash
kubectl get tenantnetworks -A
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> describe tenantnetwork <NAME>
```

```text
NAMESPACE   NAME           TENANT   NODES   READY   PHASE   AGE
acme        acme-network   acme     3       3       Ready   1m
```

| Phase      | Meaning                                                                                          |
|------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `Ready`    | The tenant network works on every selected node                                                  |
| `Pending`  | No node matches the selector, or some nodes are not ready yet                                    |
| `Error`    | The operator cannot serve the resource; `kubectl get tenantnetworks -A -o wide` shows the reason |
| `Removing` | The resource is being deleted; the operator waits for the node pods to clean up                  |

`kubectl describe` shows the state of every node and what happened recently:

```text
Status:
  Interface:      vxlan5001
  Nodes:
    worker-1:
      Overlay IP:  192.168.0.57
      State:       Ready
    worker-2:
      Message:     no tenant director answers on vxlan5001: ...
      Overlay IP:  192.168.0.58
      State:       NotReady
  Phase:          Pending
  Managed Topology:
    Key:    panfs.vdura.com/tenant-network
    Value:  acme
  Tenant:         acme
Events:
  Type     Reason               From                           Message
  Normal   SecretFound          panfs-tenant-operator  Using Secret acme-credentials; its token belongs to tenant acme
  Normal   DaemonSetCreated     panfs-tenant-operator  Created DaemonSet panfs-tenant-network-acme-network ...
  Normal   NodeAttached         panfs-tenant-network           Node worker-1 is attached to the subnet of tenant acme, overlay address 192.168.0.57/16
  Normal   InterfaceConfigured  panfs-tenant-network           Node worker-1: interface vxlan5001 was created or corrected ...
  Normal   NodeReady            panfs-tenant-network           Node worker-1: a tenant director answers on vxlan5001; the label ... is set
  Error    NodeNotReady         panfs-tenant-network           Node worker-2: no tenant director answers on vxlan5001: ...
```

An event is written when something changes, not on every check, and Kubernetes keeps events for about one hour.
A failure is an event of the type `Error`, so it stands out in `kubectl describe`. Kubernetes itself uses only
`Normal` and `Warning`: the filter `kubectl get events --types` does not accept `Error`,
so select these events with `--field-selector type=Error`.
The status always shows the current state. The pods also report on their node, as the annotations
`panfs.vdura.com/tenant-network-overlay-ip`, `-interface` and `-message`.

| Event reason                                   | Meaning                                                                |
|------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `SecretFound`                                  | The operator read the Secret and the tenant of its token               |
| `DaemonSetCreated`, `DaemonSetUpdated`         | The operator created or changed the DaemonSet of the node pods         |
| `Ready`, `Pending`, `Removing`                 | The phase changed                                                      |
| `NodeAttached`                                 | A node was attached to the subnet of the tenant                        |
| `InterfaceConfigured`, `FirewallZoneSet`       | A pod created or corrected the tenant interface of its node            |
| `NodeReady`, `NodeNotReady`                    | The check of a node passed, or failed twice in a row                   |
| `NodeStopped`                                  | A pod stopped for a restart; interface and attachment stay             |
| `NodeDetached`                                 | A node left the tenant and was detached                                |
| `NodeLabelRemoved`                             | The operator removed a ready label that the pods left behind           |
| `InvalidSpec`, `SecretNotFound`, `PanAPIError` | Error: the operator cannot serve the resource                          |
| `TenantAlreadyServed`, `NodeSelectorOverlap`   | Error: another `TenantNetwork` has the tenant, the namespace or a node |
| `ReconcileFailed`                              | Error: the operator cannot serve the resource                          |

For more detail, read the log of the pod of a node:

```bash
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> logs -l panfs.vdura.com/tenant-network-name=<NAME> --tail=20
```

### 5. Create the StorageClass of the tenant

A StorageClass of the tenant is a `PanfsStorageClass` in the namespace of the `TenantNetwork`.
With Helm, it is one release of the chart [`panfs-storageclass`](../storageclass/Readme.md).
The name of the release is the name of the StorageClass, and no value is needed:

```bash
helm upgrade --install acme-storage ../storageclass \
    --namespace acme
```

The PanFS parameters and the other settings of the StorageClass are values of that chart, for example
`--set reclaimPolicy=Retain`; see its [values](../storageclass/Readme.md#values).

The chart creates a `PanfsStorageClass`. Without Helm, apply the same resource yourself. The smallest one is only a name:

```yaml
apiVersion: panfs.vdura.com/v1alpha1
kind: PanfsStorageClass
metadata:
  name: acme-storage
  namespace: acme
```

The template [template-panfsstorageclass.yaml](../../deploy/k8s/storage-class/template-panfsstorageclass.yaml)
shows all fields.

In both ways, the operator finds the `TenantNetwork` by itself, because a namespace holds the network of one tenant.
It creates the StorageClass `acme-storage`. It fills in the provisioner, the references to the Secret
of the `TenantNetwork`, and `allowedTopologies` with the ready label of the tenant network
(`panfs.vdura.com/tenant-network=<NAMESPACE>`, which the `TenantNetwork` names in `status.managedTopology`).
It also lets the CSI controller read the Secret.

```bash
kubectl get panfsstorageclasses -A
```

```text
NAMESPACE   NAME           STORAGECLASS   TENANT-NETWORK   PHASE   AGE
acme        acme-storage   acme-storage   acme             Ready   1m
```

Every volume of this StorageClass is bound to the nodes where the tenant network works: its PersistentVolume
gets the ready label as node affinity. Kubernetes then places every pod that uses the volume on a ready node.
A workload needs no node selector. The resource is described in [PanfsStorageClass](#panfsstorageclass).

> **Note:** the CSI driver reports topology. A StorageClass that you write yourself, with `allowedTopologies`
> on the ready label, must have `volumeBindingMode: Immediate`. With `WaitForFirstConsumer` no volume is provisioned:
> the claim shows the event `error generating accessibility requirements`.

### More than one tenant

Create one Secret and one `TenantNetwork` per tenant, each tenant in its own namespace, and label each node for one tenant.
A node without the label gets no pod, and nothing on it changes.

### Adding and removing nodes

- A new node gets a pod as soon as it matches the `nodeSelector` of the `TenantNetwork`.
- When you remove that label from a node, or change `nodeSelector`, Kubernetes deletes the pods of the nodes that no longer match. Before it stops, the pod removes the ready label,
  removes the tenant interface, and detaches the node from the subnet in the realm.
  Move the pods that use volumes of the tenant away from the node first: without the interface their volumes stop answering.

### Removing the network of a tenant

Remove the workloads that use volumes of the tenant first. Then delete the `TenantNetwork`:

```bash
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> delete tenantnetwork <NAME>
```

The delete waits until the operator has removed the DaemonSet and every pod has cleaned up its node:
the ready label, the tenant interface, and the attachment in the realm are gone afterwards.

Delete the `TenantNetwork` before you delete its namespace or its Secret. When the whole namespace is deleted
at once, the pods can lose their permissions before they have cleaned up. A node is detached only when its pod
can see for certain that the node left the tenant, so the following can stay behind:

- **The ready label.** The operator removes it: before it releases a deleted `TenantNetwork`, and in every pass
  for a label whose namespace has no `TenantNetwork` any more. Workloads are not sent to such a node.
- **The tenant interface and the attachment in the realm.** The operator cannot remove them, because it does not
  run on the node and the token is gone with the Secret. Remove the interface with `ip link delete <INTERFACE>`
  on the node, and the attachment in the realm.

The delete also waits for a node that cannot be reached, for example one that is powered off: its pod cannot
confirm that it stopped, so the `TenantNetwork` stays in the phase `Removing`. Bring the node back, or delete
its Node object (`kubectl delete node <NODE_NAME>`) when the node is gone for good.

To keep the interfaces and the attachments, set `detachOnRemoval` to `false` before you delete:

```bash
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> patch tenantnetwork <NAME> --type merge -p '{"spec":{"detachOnRemoval":false}}'
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> rollout status daemonset/panfs-tenant-network-<NAME>
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> delete tenantnetwork <NAME>
```

## PanfsStorageClass

### Fields

Every field is optional in a namespace with a `TenantNetwork`, and `spec` itself can be left out.

| Field                       | Default   | Meaning                                                                        |
|:----------------------------|:----------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `spec.realmSecretName`      |           | Name of a Secret in the same namespace, for a realm without a tenant network   |
| `spec.parameters`           | none      | PanFS parameters of new volumes; without them the defaults of the driver apply |
| `spec.reclaimPolicy`        | `Delete`  | `Delete` or `Retain`                                                           |
| `spec.volumeBindingMode`    | see below | `Immediate` or `WaitForFirstConsumer`                                          |
| `spec.allowVolumeExpansion` | `true`    | Whether a volume can be expanded                                               |
| `spec.mountOptions`         | none      | Mount options of the volumes                                                   |
| `spec.default`              | `false`   | Whether the StorageClass is the default one of the cluster                     |

The parameters are listed in the [StorageClass chart](../storageclass/Readme.md#panfs-specific-volume-parameters),
for example `panfs.csi.vdura.com/layout`. They reach the StorageClass unchanged. The parameters that start with
`csi.storage.k8s.io/` are set by the operator and are refused in the resource.

The operator decides by the namespace of the resource where the Secret and the nodes come from:

| The namespace has  | `spec.realmSecretName` | Result                                                             |
|:-------------------|:-----------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| a `TenantNetwork`  | not set                | The Secret and the nodes of the `TenantNetwork`                    |
| a `TenantNetwork`  | set                    | Refused: the volumes would not be bound to the nodes of the tenant |
| no `TenantNetwork` | set                    | That Secret; the volumes are not bound to nodes                    |
| no `TenantNetwork` | not set                | Error until a `TenantNetwork` exists or the field is set           |

`status.tenantNetwork` names the `TenantNetwork` that the operator found.

What the operator fills in:

| In the StorageClass                                        | Comes from                                                   |
|:-----------------------------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------|
| `provisioner`                                              | Always `com.vdura.csi.panfs`                                 |
| `csi.storage.k8s.io/*-secret-name` and `-secret-namespace` | The Secret of the `TenantNetwork`, or `spec.realmSecretName` |
| `allowedTopologies`                                        | `status.managedTopology` of the `TenantNetwork`              |

### Volume binding mode

With a tenant network the mode is always `Immediate`: a volume is created as soon as its PersistentVolumeClaim exists,
and it is bound to the nodes of the tenant network. `WaitForFirstConsumer` is refused in a namespace with a `TenantNetwork`.
The provisioner compares `allowedTopologies` with the labels that the CSI node plugin reported when a node is
already chosen, and the ready label of the tenant network is not one of them, so no volume would be created.

With `realmSecretName` the default is `WaitForFirstConsumer`, and the StorageClass has no `allowedTopologies`.

### A realm without a tenant network

When the nodes reach the realm without a tenant network, name the Secret. The namespace must have no `TenantNetwork`:

```yaml
apiVersion: panfs.vdura.com/v1alpha1
kind: PanfsStorageClass
metadata:
  name: panfs-storage
  namespace: panfs-realm
spec:
  realmSecretName: realm-credentials
```

The Secret has the keys `api` and `token`, and optionally `ca_cert`. The volumes are not bound to nodes.
With Helm, see [Realm without a tenant network](../storageclass/Readme.md#realm-without-a-tenant-network)
of the StorageClass chart.

Such a cluster installs the whole operator, although it needs only this part of it: the tenant network part
finds no `TenantNetwork` and does nothing.

### Status

| Phase     | Meaning                                                                                                   |
|:----------|:----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `Ready`   | The StorageClass is in place                                                                              |
| `Pending` | The `TenantNetwork` is not ready yet; the StorageClass is created when it is                              |
| `Error`   | The message says what is wrong: a missing `TenantNetwork` or Secret, a name that is taken, a refused edit |

No StorageClass is created while the phase is `Pending`, or while it is `Error` for a new resource.
A StorageClass that exists stays when its
`TenantNetwork` is not ready for a while, for example while a node restarts; the message then says so.
The operator reports changes as events on the resource: `kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> describe panfsstorageclass <NAME>`.

### The name is unique in the cluster

A StorageClass has no namespace, and it takes the name of the resource. When two resources in two namespaces
have one name, the one whose StorageClass is created first keeps it, and the other one shows an error. The operator also refuses
a name that a StorageClass of somebody else has: it never takes over a StorageClass that it did not create.

### Changing a PanfsStorageClass

Kubernetes does not allow a change of the parameters, the reclaim policy or the volume binding mode of a StorageClass.
The operator therefore refuses a change of `spec.parameters`, `spec.reclaimPolicy`, `spec.volumeBindingMode`
or `spec.realmSecretName`, and a change of the Secret that the `TenantNetwork` names: the StorageClass stays as it is, and the resource shows the phase
`Error` with the name of the field. Undo the change, or delete the resource and create it again.
Existing volumes keep what they were created with.

A change of `spec.allowVolumeExpansion`, `spec.mountOptions` or `spec.default` is applied to the StorageClass.

### Deleting a PanfsStorageClass

```bash
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> delete panfsstorageclass <NAME>
```

The operator deletes the StorageClass. Existing volumes keep working, and their pods are still placed on the
nodes of the tenant network. While a volume of the StorageClass exists, the `Role` and the `RoleBinding`
`panfs-storage-class-<NAME>` stay: the CSI controller reads the Secret to expand and to delete a volume.
An event on the resource says so. Delete the two by hand when the volumes are gone:

```bash
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> delete role,rolebinding panfs-storage-class-<NAME>
```

When you create a resource with the same name again, it uses the same `Role`. The operator adds the Secret
of the new resource to it and keeps the Secret of the old volumes.

### The ServiceAccount of the CSI controller

The `RoleBinding` names the ServiceAccount of the CSI controller. The operator reads the namespace of the driver
from the CSIDriver object `com.vdura.csi.panfs`, and it uses the name that the driver chart gives the account:
`<namespace of the driver>-controller`. For a driver that was installed in another way, set the chart values
`csiDriverNamespace` and `csiControllerServiceAccount`.

### What a volume keeps for its whole life

The node affinity of a PersistentVolume cannot be changed. A volume of a StorageClass with a tenant network
keeps the label `panfs.vdura.com/tenant-network=<NAMESPACE>`, with the namespace of the `TenantNetwork`.
Do not move a `TenantNetwork` that has volumes to another namespace: the pods of its volumes could then
not be placed on any node.

### Moving a StorageClass of the old StorageClass chart

Up to version 1.2.6 the chart `csi-panfs-storageclass` created a StorageClass and a Secret itself.
That chart is gone: from version 2.0.0 on, the [StorageClass chart](../storageclass/Readme.md) creates
a `PanfsStorageClass`. A StorageClass that exists keeps working. To move it to a `PanfsStorageClass`:

1. Keep the Secret. A Helm uninstall deletes the Secret of the chart, so create a Secret of your own with the
   same keys first (`api`, `token`, and `ca_cert` if you use it), or use the Secret of the
   [tenant chart](../tenant/Readme.md).
2. Choose a new name for the `PanfsStorageClass`. The operator does not take over the StorageClass of the chart,
   and the parameters of a StorageClass cannot be changed.
3. Create the `PanfsStorageClass` with the `parameters`, `reclaimPolicy`, `mountOptions` and
   `allowVolumeExpansion` of the old StorageClass. The values of the old chart map like this:

   - `parameters` → `spec.parameters`
   - `volumeReclaimPolicy` → `spec.reclaimPolicy`
   - `volumeBindingMode` → `spec.volumeBindingMode` (left out with a tenant network)
   - `allowVolumeExpansion` → `spec.allowVolumeExpansion`
   - `mountOptions` → `spec.mountOptions`
   - `setAsDefaultStorageClass` → `spec.default`
   - `allowedTopologies` → not needed: the operator takes it from the `TenantNetwork` of the namespace
   - `realm.*` → the Secret
   - `csiPanFSDriver.namespace` → not needed: the operator reads it from the CSIDriver object
4. Use the new name in `storageClassName` of new PersistentVolumeClaims.
5. Existing volumes keep working without their StorageClass. They have no node affinity, so their workloads
   keep the node selector `panfs.vdura.com/tenant-network: "<NAMESPACE>"`. When no new volume needs the old
   StorageClass any more, remove the Helm release, but keep the Secret of step 1 and a `Role` that lets
   the CSI controller read it while old volumes exist.

> **Note:** from version 2.0.0 on, the CSI driver reports topology. A StorageClass that you wrote yourself with
> `volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer` and with `allowedTopologies` on the ready label of the tenant network
> no longer provisions volumes: the claim shows the event `error generating accessibility requirements`.
> Replace it with a `PanfsStorageClass`, or create it again with `volumeBindingMode: Immediate`.

## Moving from the two operators to the one

Development builds of version 2.0.0 had two operators: the PanFS Tenant Network operator (chart
`panfs-tenant-network-operator`, image `panfs-tenant-network`) and the PanFS StorageClass operator (chart
`panfs-storageclass-operator`, image `panfs-storageclass-operator`). No release had them. A cluster that runs them
moves to the PanFS Tenant operator like this. The `TenantNetwork` and `PanfsStorageClass` resources, the StorageClasses,
the volumes and the tenant interfaces on the nodes all stay in place.

1. Stop the two old operators, but keep their releases for now. The `ClusterRole` of the node pods stays this way,
   so the node pods keep working:

   ```bash
   kubectl -n panfs-tenant-network scale deployment panfs-tenant-network-operator --replicas=0
   kubectl -n csi-panfs scale deployment panfs-storageclass-operator --replicas=0
   ```

   Use the namespaces and the release names of your cluster. Two operators that serve one resource at the same time
   would undo the changes of each other, so do not skip this step.

2. Install the PanFS Tenant operator; see [Install the operator](#1-install-the-operator). The two resource
   definitions are already in the cluster, and Helm leaves them as they are.

3. Wait until the operator has taken over. It moves the DaemonSet of every `TenantNetwork` to its own image and
   binds the node pods to its own `ClusterRole`. The node pods restart one node at a time; a restart does not
   remove the tenant interface, so mounted volumes keep working.

   ```bash
   kubectl get tenantnetworks,panfsstorageclasses -A
   kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> rollout status daemonset/panfs-tenant-network-<NAME>
   ```

   Every resource must show the phase `Ready` again.

4. Remove the two old releases, and the old namespace when nothing else is in it:

   ```bash
   helm uninstall panfs-tenant-network-operator --namespace panfs-tenant-network
   helm uninstall panfs-storageclass-operator --namespace csi-panfs
   kubectl delete namespace panfs-tenant-network
   ```

   Do not delete the resource definitions: the new operator uses them.

5. A release of the former chart `panfs-tenant-storageclass` needs no change. The chart is now
   [`panfs-storageclass`](../storageclass/Readme.md) in `charts/storageclass`, with the same values;
   use that path for the next `helm upgrade` of the release.

The images `panfs-tenant-network` and `panfs-storageclass-operator` are no longer built.

## Uninstalling the operator

Delete every `PanfsStorageClass` and every `TenantNetwork` first, while the operator runs. Then:

```bash
helm uninstall panfs-tenant-operator --namespace csi-panfs
kubectl delete crd tenantnetworks.panfs.vdura.com panfsstorageclasses.panfs.vdura.com
```

Helm does not remove or upgrade a `CustomResourceDefinition`, so the last command is yours.
After an upgrade of the chart, apply the new definitions by hand: `kubectl apply -f crds/`.

Do not uninstall the operator while a `TenantNetwork` exists. The uninstall removes the `ClusterRole` of the node pods,
so the pods can no longer set or remove the ready label; the interfaces and the attachments stay as they are.
A `TenantNetwork` or a `PanfsStorageClass` also cannot be deleted then: it waits for the operator.
If the operator is gone for good, clean up by hand:

```bash
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> delete daemonset panfs-tenant-network-<NAME> --cascade=foreground
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> patch tenantnetwork <NAME> --type merge -p '{"metadata":{"finalizers":null}}'
kubectl delete clusterrolebinding <NAMESPACE>-panfs-tenant-network-<NAME>

kubectl delete storageclass <STORAGE_CLASS_NAME>
kubectl -n <NAMESPACE> patch panfsstorageclass <STORAGE_CLASS_NAME> --type merge -p '{"metadata":{"finalizers":null}}'
```

